---
import Legal from "../layouts/Legal.astro";
import { CONTACT_EMAIL, REPO } from "../lib/site";
---

<Legal
  title="Privacy policy"
  description="Warren is self-hosted. The apps connect to your own Warren. Desktop update checks contact GitHub, and optional cloud setup contacts Hetzner. The project collects no analytics."
  current="privacy"
>
  <div class="note">
    <p><strong>The short version.</strong></p>
    <ul>
      <li>Warren runs on your own computer or server, which we call your <em>hub</em>. We don't run a Warren cloud.</li>
      <li>The Warren apps connect to your hub. Desktop update checks also contact GitHub, and cloud setup contacts Hetzner if you use it.</li>
      <li>The Warren project collects no analytics, no tracking data and no personal information. None of it reaches us.</li>
      <li>Your AI agents send what they work on to the AI services you choose to set up, such as Anthropic or OpenAI.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <h2>Who this covers</h2>
  <p>
    This policy covers Warren, the free and open-source software made by the Warren project, including the desktop app,
    the phone apps, the web app and this website. In this policy, "we" means the people who make Warren. "You" means
    anyone who uses it.
  </p>

  <h2>Warren is self-hosted</h2>
  <p>
    When you use Warren, everything lives on a hub: a computer or server that you, or someone you trust, runs. Your
    messages, your agents, the memory vault, your files and your account (your name, email address and a scrambled form
    of your password) are stored on that hub and nowhere else. We can't see any of it, and we have no way to get it.
  </p>
  <p>
    If you join a Warren that someone else runs, like your company or a friend, that person runs the hub and decides how
    your information there is kept. Ask them about it.
  </p>

  <h2>What the apps do</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>They connect to your hub.</strong> The desktop, iPhone and Android apps connect to the hub address you
      give them. Desktop update checks and downloads contact GitHub. If you use the desktop app's cloud setup,
      it contacts Hetzner to create and manage your server. Push notifications use the services described below.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>No analytics, ads or trackers.</strong> The apps contain no analytics tools, advertising tools or crash
      reporters that send data to us or anyone else.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Sign-in stays on your device.</strong> The phone apps keep the sign-in for your hub in your phone's secure
      storage (the Keychain on iPhone, the app's private storage on Android). Sign out in the app and revoke its session on your hub to remove access.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>The camera is only for pairing.</strong> To pair your phone, you scan a QR code shown in Warren on your
      computer (<strong>Settings → Mobile</strong>). The app uses the camera only for that, and doesn't save or send any
      pictures. On Android, the code is read by the scanner built into Google Play services on your phone. The code
      holds your hub's address and a one-time pairing secret.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Push notifications</h2>
  <p>
    If you turn on notifications in the iPhone or iPad app, your hub sends them through Apple's push service. (The
    Android app doesn't have notifications yet.) The hub uses push keys that belong to whoever runs the hub, not to us. Your
    phone gives your hub a push token, which your hub keeps. A notification's text, such as the start of a message,
    passes through Apple on its way to your phone, under Apple's privacy policy. You can turn notifications
    off at any time in the app or in your phone's settings.
  </p>

  <h2>AI services you choose</h2>
  <p>
    Your agents are AI tools such as Claude Code, Codex, Hermes or Gemini CLI. To do their work, they send what you ask
    them, and the information they need for it (for example messages, notes, files or what's on their computer), to the
    AI service they're set up with, using the account or key you give them. That data goes straight from your computers
    to that service, under that service's own privacy policy. It doesn't go through us. Choose services you trust, and
    read their policies.
  </p>
  <p>
    The same goes for apps you connect to Warren, like your email, calendar or Notion: your hub talks to them directly,
    and their own policies apply.
  </p>

  <h2>This website</h2>
  <p>
    This website is a set of plain pages. It doesn't use cookies, analytics or trackers, and it doesn't load anything
    from other companies. Like any website, the company that hosts it (Cloudflare) may briefly keep standard technical
    logs, such as IP addresses, to keep the site running and safe. Downloads come from GitHub, under{" "}
    <a href="https://docs.github.com/site-policy/privacy-policies/github-general-privacy-statement" rel="noopener"
      >GitHub's privacy statement</a
    >.
  </p>

  <h2>Children</h2>
  <p>
    Warren isn't made for children under 13, and we don't knowingly collect information from anyone, including children.
  </p>

  <h2>Your choices</h2>
  <p>
    Because your information lives on your hub, you control it there. The person who runs a hub can see, change, export
    and delete what's stored on it, including your account. Removing the app alone does not delete a computer's hub
    data or services. For complete deletion on a computer, run <a href={`${REPO}/blob/main/uninstall.sh`}>uninstall.sh</a>
    and confirm the relevant removal steps. Remove the phone app and revoke its sign-in on your hub to disconnect it.
    Since we don't hold your data, there's nothing for us to hand over or delete, but we're happy to answer questions.
  </p>

  <h2>Open source</h2>
  <p>
    Warren's code is public, so anyone can check what it does. You can read it on <a href={REPO} rel="noopener">GitHub</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Changes</h2>
  <p>
    If this policy changes, we'll update this page and the date at the top. If a change matters, we'll also mention it in
    Warren's release notes.
  </p>

  <h2>Contact</h2>
  <p>Questions about privacy? Email <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}`}>{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a>.</p>
</Legal>
